## Expected engine lines in the router audit's log (deliberate, not faults)

```
WARNING: ScreenRouter.go_to: screen "menu" is not registered; staying on ""
WARNING: ScreenRouter.go_to: screen "nope" is not registered; staying on "modes"
WARNING: ScreenRouter.go_to: screen "screen-menu" is not registered; staying on "modes"
ERROR: ScreenRouter.register: 'nope' is not one of the thirteen reference screens
ERROR: ScreenRouter.register: 'menu' was handed a null scene
```

Those five lines are the audit exercising the refusal paths on purpose: `go_to` before
any registration, `go_to` with an unknown id, `go_to` with a DOM id instead of a router
id, `register` with an id outside the thirteen, and `register` with a null scene. Each
one is also asserted (`go_to` returns false, the active screen does not change, no
`screen_changed` is emitted, `register` returns false). There are no other `ERROR:`,
`WARNING:` or script-error lines in either command's log.

## What the 86 checks cover

| Group | Checks | What is proven |
|---|---|---|
| table | 24 | exactly thirteen ids; equal to `nav_routes.gd`'s `ROUTE_SCREENS` ids (normalized over the `screen-` prefix) and to `ROUTE_REGISTRY`, both ways; the DOM anchors are the reference ids in document order; `dom_id_of`/`id_of_dom` round-trip and answer `""` for anything unknown; the declared back target of every screen matches the reference's `data-back`; exactly menu/game/result declare none; the `to-*` action lists match the reference's own; the result carries `to-menu`, the field carries none |
| registration and mounting | 20 | a fresh router mounts nothing and refuses `go_to`; thirteen scenes register; `register` refuses an id outside the table and a null scene; `go_to` mounts exactly the screen it names, exactly one at a time; the outgoing screen leaves the tree before the next enters and is queued for deletion; the last transition stands; one `screen_changed` per transition with the right `from`/`to`; the payload hands the screen its `router_id` and `back_target` and keeps a caller's keys; an unknown id (and a DOM id) is refused with the active screen untouched and no signal |
| shell, in a real 1280x720 frame | 19 | the shell scene loads; `screen_id`, title and subtitle resolve through the locale seam (the title is a sentence, not an id); the back control shows only where a return is declared, is named `<screen_id>/back`, carries the `back` action, and its registration is dropped when it is hidden and restored when re-declared; a content control registers under `<screen_id>/<suffix>`; re-registering a suffix replaces rather than stacks; the safe-area margin comes from the shell's own constant; every one of the thirteen screens lays out full-frame inside the frame it is mounted in, one at a time |
| placeholder and contract | 8 | the placeholder reports the id it was handed, says what it is, builds its shell, declares `["default"]` as its capture state, and the base class refuses to apply a state it was not told about |
| UiStrings | 5 | the fallback chain answers with the id itself for an unknown key, `has()` is false for the same id and true for `back`/`modesTitle` |
| source rules | 7 | the router does not preload the reference inventory (its own copy is compared, never shared), names it for the reader, carries the freeze note; `UiStrings.gd` owns no table, does not re-declare the locale list and delegates to `res://src/locale/locale.gd`; the contract cites `js/ui.js:595-607` and the blank-page fix `js/ui.js:596-598` |
| literal scan | 4 | see below |

## The literal scan (the DoD's "no user-facing literal" rule, with its own proof)

```
# report literal scan scanned=4 files offenders=0 exemptions=1 whitelist=1
# note the scan's rule: a string literal containing a space, outside a comment and not
#      on a push_error/push_warning/printerr/assert line, is prose; the whitelist above
#      is the only exception
```

- Files scanned (4): `res://src/ui/ScreenRouter.gd`, `res://src/ui/ScreenShell.gd`,
  `res://src/ui/PlaceholderScreen.gd`, `res://src/ui/screens/ScreenContract.gd`.
- Exempted (1, by path with its reason in the audit): `res://src/ui/UiStrings.gd` — it
  is the seam itself and owns no table; its doc comment quotes ids to state the chain.
- Whitelisted (1, by path **and** literal, never by file): `not built yet` in
  `PlaceholderScreen.gd` — the ticket's own label for a screen whose ticket has not
  landed; the screen asks `UiStrings.has("ui_placeholder_not_built")` first, so the day
  a key exists the literal stops being used.
- Offenders: **0**.
- The scan is proved non-vacuous before it is trusted: against the synthetic source
  `PROBE_SOURCE` (a comment quoting prose, a prose label, a `push_error("dev only")` and
  a locale id) it returns exactly `["res://probe.gd:2 \"Play now\""]`
  (`ok router/the_literal_scan_flags_prose_and_ignores_developer_text`).

